  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe weather pattern for Friday and Saturday will be similar to \nyesterday, with the Bermuda high pushing warm humid air into the \nregion and a broad trough over Canada slinging shortwaves through \nthe region. \n\nOn Friday, heat and humidity will build as the day progresses, with \nhighs in the mid to upper 80s and dewpoints back in the upper 60s to \nlow 70s. Rain and thunderstorms are expected to develop again. \nPlenty of instability should be present with pockets of 1500-2000 \nj/kg of CAPE across the area, but shear is expected to be weaker \nwith 10-25kts of 0-6km bulk shear expected. The trigger for  these \nshowers and storms will be a shortwave moving in from the Great \nLakes. Latest guidance has slowed this feature down compared to \nprevious forecasts, with it arriving later in the afternoon. CAMs \nhave caught on to this and are showing precip initiation starting \naround mid afternoon, lasting into the late evening hours. SPC has \nportions of the Catskills and NEPA in a Marginal Risk for severe \nstorms. Based on the current ingredients forecast, it seems the best \nchance for severe weather will from a cell that is able to tap into \na pocket of higher CAPE and have a collapsing core cause a \nmicroburst. \n\nHeavy rain will be accompany some of these storms, allowing for a \nchance for isolated flash flooding across areas that have been \nsaturated from the recent rains. WPC has put our entire area \nin a Marginal risk for flash flooding.\n\nOn Saturday, another weak shortwave will ripple through the area, \nkicking off another round of showers and storms in the afternoon and \nevening. The severe threat looks very low as bulk shear is forecast \nto be even weaker and CAPE a little weaker. Heavy rain chances will \nremain, with isolated flash flooding possible across areas\nsaturated by recent rains. A Marginal Risk for flash flooding \ncontinues across the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA welcome reprieve from heat and rain is expected on Sunday as weak \nhigh pressure builds into the area. Temps in the upper 70s to mid \n80s are expected, but the much lower humidity will make it feel \nquite nice outside. \n\nA weather pattern change starting at the beginning of next week will \nbring continued shower chances through the week, but temperatures \nand humidity will be trending lower. The Bermuda high is expected to \nweaken and slide south over the Bahamas into the Gulf as more zonal \nflow develops across the Great Lakes into the Northeast. This will \nend the southerly flow pushing the higher humidity into the area and \nallow for Canadian air to filter into the region, bringing cooler \ntemperatures through the week. A few shortwaves will ripple through \nthe pattern, kicking off scattered showers through mid week. A \nstronger shortwave with a cold front is progged to move through the \narea sometime during the end of the week, bringing high pressure, \ndry conditions and cooler temps for the weekend.",
